<!--
  Workspace Playground — MyStory
  FQN: org.blockml.bml.workspace.playground.MyStory
-->
<block
  xmlns="http://blockml.org/bml"
  xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
  xsi:schemaLocation="http://blockml.org/bml https://blockml.org/schemas/bml.xsd
                      "
  xmlns:core="org.blockml.bml.core"
  xmlns:type="org.blockml.bml.type"
  xmlns:playground="org.blockml.bml.workspace.playground">

  <name>MyStory</name>
  <type>playground:MyStory</type>
  <baseType>core:Documentation</baseType>
  <is>A random structured story produced by a workspace transform execution, in English, about a squirrel and a mouse</is>

  <documentation>
    A structured English story about a squirrel and a mouse. The story text and its
    metadata are carried as typed properties. Each transform execution replaces
    this block with a freshly composed story.
  </documentation>

  <properties>
    <title type="type:Text">
      <value>The Acorn Ledger</value>
    </title>
    <author type="type:Text">
      <value>John Doe</value>
    </author>
    <story type="type:Text">
      <value>High in the split trunk of an old beech, a red squirrel named Pindle kept what he proudly called his ledger: a hollow packed with acorns, each one counted twice before winter. He trusted the count more than he trusted the weather.

One grey afternoon a house mouse climbed the beech, shaking rain from her whiskers. "You have more acorns than a squirrel can chew," she said. "I am Tamsin. I would trade you three fat hazelnuts for one."

"Trade?" Pindle bristled. "I do not trade. I hoard. That is the whole point of a ledger."

Tamsin sat down on a knot of bark, unbothered. "A ledger only helps if you can read it in the snow. Last year the frost came early and your cousins forgot half their holes. The forest ate their savings."

Pindle's tail twitched, because this was true, and truths that arrive with wet whiskers are the hardest to argue with. "Suppose I did trade," he said slowly. "What would a mouse do with a single acorn?"

"Plant it," said Tamsin. "By the stream, where you never dig. In ten years it is an oak, and an oak is a bank that pays every autumn, to every small creature that asks."

They argued until the rain stopped, and then, mostly to end the argument, Pindle rolled one acorn out of the hollow. Tamsin took it in both paws, thanked him gravely, and carried it down to the soft ground by the water.

Pindle told himself he had lost an acorn. But that night he slept badly, and in the morning he found himself counting a different way — not how many he had kept, but how many he could afford to give. By spring a green shoot stood by the stream, and Pindle, who still called himself a hoarder, visited it more often than he would admit.</value>
    </story>
    <language type="type:Text">
      <value>English</value>
    </language>
    <date type="type:DateTime">
      <value>2026-09-04T00:00:00</value>
    </date>
    <time type="type:DateTime">
      <value>2026-09-04T01:49:02</value>
    </time>
    <location type="type:Text">
      <value>A hollow beech at the edge of Wistman's Wood, Dartmoor, England</value>
    </location>
    <weather type="type:Text">
      <value>Overcast and cool, with a steady autumn drizzle easing to mist by late afternoon</value>
    </weather>
  </properties>

</block>
